      "notes": "§7.6 (Phase 4b): `history purge` on a repository whose capture is off removes everything the recorder holds for it. Captured: `a.md` (c_0, r_0; it links `b.md`, e_0 opened at c_0), `b.md` (c_1, r_1), the edit of `a.md` (c_2, r_2 — a delta on r_0). `history disable` at 10:03 closes the interval. The purge at 10:04 deletes the three `commits`, three `revisions`, every blob and tree node (nothing reaches them), the dispositions and block changes, and the one open edge transition (no commit remains to anchor it); the recorder's hold leaves every claim it was on (`claims_released` counts them — the docs, heads, blocks, commits and the edge; capture is off, so the live set is not kept) and, no repository being enabled with every history table empty, the bundle's tables are DROPPED (`tables_dropped: true`): the projection shows the history tables `[]` and `id_claims` with the core's bit alone. Coverage is untouched (`[{ from: <birth>, to: 10:03 }]` — the record of when capture was on, which is what makes a purged token read `pruned` at the surface). A second purge finds nothing (every count 0, nothing dropped). The write after it (`b.md` edited, c_3) lands on the core alone.",
