      "name": "purge-before-trims-an-enabled-repository",
      "notes": "§7.6 (Phase 4b): `history purge --before <ts>` on a repository whose capture is ON trims the past and keeps the present. `a.md` is observed three times (c_0 r_0, c_1 r_1, c_2 r_2 — r_1 and r_2 deltas on their predecessors; `a.md` links `b.md`, e_0 opened at c_0) and `b.md` once (c_3, r_3). The purge at 10:05 before 10:02:30 has candidates c_0, c_1, c_2; c_2 is PROTECTED (it holds the live head r_2) so c_0 and c_1 go with r_0 and r_1, their dispositions and block changes. Before the rows go, r_2 — whose base r_1 is purged — is re-written in CHECKPOINT form (the squash's rule, §4.4), so `read_at r_2` still reconstructs byte for byte; the swept blobs/tree nodes are the bodies only r_0/r_1 reached. The open transition e_0 opened at the purged c_0 is RE-ANCHORED at the earliest retained commit c_2 (H6 holds: every open edge has an open transition). The recorder's hold is released on r_0 and r_1 alone (`claims_released` 2: their claims drop to the core's bit); the live set — d_0, d_1, the heads r_2/r_3, the live blocks, e_0 — keeps the hold as a baseline would (I7 holds). Nothing is dropped (the repository is enabled). Coverage is untouched. The edit after the purge (c_4, r_4) is captured as a delta on the re-checkpointed r_2; `read_at r_0` finds no row (`target_missing` at the store level — the surface reads it `pruned`, spec/surface §2.1).",
